--- Finding (rational_calculator): a record made by a call that can fail (its
--- argument can: int[text], fraction[n, d]) written into a record-or-none field
--- types the field as one that can fail, `Result(OptRecord(Node))`, and then a 0
--- written into that field elsewhere (the none) is not made a none: the compiled
--- program does not build. Found building an expression tree of fractions
--- (`Node['+', fraction[0, 1], num[fraction[1, 3]], …]` with `fn num[q]` /
--- `return Node['num', q, 0, 0]`).
---
--- crow run -i:  2
--- crow run:     error[E0308]: mismatched types --> src/main.rs
---               error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler
---               refused what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program
--- Expected: 2 in both modes.
---
--- `crow build --ir`: `struct Node { value: Big, left: Result(OptRecord(Node)) }`
--- and `return Node{q, 0}` — the 0 goes in as the literal (`value: 0_i64` in a
--- `CrowResult<Option<Link<Node>>>`), where a field typed `OptRecord(Node)`
--- has it through `core.__none`. Fine: the inner record made in place
--- (`Node[1, Node[int['2'], 0]]` types `left: OptRecord(Node)`), and an argument
--- that cannot fail (`leaf[2]`). Related to the fixed "a record from a call that
--- can fail handed where a link is taken": here the field is also given 0.
struct Node [value, left]

fn leaf[q]
    return Node[q, 0]

tree = Node[1, leaf[int['2']]]
log[tree.left.value]
